#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbdcd5f-41de-52fa-9652-3084a5edc01f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Second Term of a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "78ec85fc-7ed5-5486-bc84-27751b7f70de" :question/math-academy-id "e-14369" :question/problem "If $a_{n+1}=2a_n-3$ with $a_1=4,$ then find the value of $a_2.$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1}=2a_n - 3\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1={\\color{blue}4}.$\n\nWe compute $a_2$ by substituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =2{a}_{n}-3 \\\\ {a}_{1+1} & =2{a}_{1}-3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(4)-3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_2=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36f5a6d-8206-5d0f-a3b9-e42c179c0452" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third Term of a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "e39ea126-770e-5556-9b16-6a2563c271d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14373" :question/problem "Given the recursive sequence $a_{n+1} = 3a_n-2$ with $a_1=2,$ what is the value of $a_3?$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3a_n-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1={\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nWe use this formula to compute the terms $a_2$ and $a_3$ as follows:\n\n- We first compute $a_2$ by substituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =3{a}_{n}-2 \\\\ {a}_{1+1} & =3{a}_{1}-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3{a}_{1}-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3(2)-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $a_3$ by substituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =3{a}_{n}-2 \\\\ {a}_{2+1} & =3{a}_{2}-2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3{a}_{2}-2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3(4)-2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_3=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74655b8-92bc-5142-96b7-408643c8ad39" :knowledge-point/title "Recursive Sequences in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "529c3240-d3a0-5740-a868-dc76f0be5fff" :question/math-academy-id "e-14377" :question/problem "If $f(n+1) = 2f(n) + 1$ with $f(1) = 3,$ then what is  $f(4)?$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = 2f(n) + 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(1) = {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nWe use this formula to compute the terms $f(2), f(3),$ and $f(4)$ as follows:\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we substitute $n = 1$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =2f(n)+1 \\\\ f(1+1) & =2f(1)+1 \\\\ f(2) & =2f(1)+1 \\\\ f(2) & =2(3)+1 \\\\ f(2) & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(3),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =2f(n)+1 \\\\ f(2+1) & =2f(2)+1 \\\\ f(3) & =2f(2)+1 \\\\ f(3) & =2(7)+1 \\\\ f(3) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=3$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =2f(n)+1 \\\\ f(3+1) & =2f(3)+1 \\\\ f(4) & =2f(3)+1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(15)+1 \\\\ f(4) & =31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4) = 31.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a1bf68a-bdb3-5e76-a587-ff0abdae982f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Recursive Formula for a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "86382a53-f250-529c-9980-840d7a614e2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14379" :question/problem "Consider the following sequence: \n\n$$\n1, \\quad 6, \\quad 11, \\quad 16, \\quad 21, \\quad 26, \\quad \\dots\n$$\n\nThe recursive formula for this sequence is given by \n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,, \\qquad f(1) = \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the first term of the sequence is $f(1) = {\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nInspecting the terms of the sequence, we see that we always get the next term by adding ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\nTherefore, the recursive rule must be the following:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{{\\color{red}5}}\\,, \\qquad f(1) = \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{{\\color{blue}1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing values are $5$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36f5a6d-8206-5d0f-a3b9-e42c179c0452" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third Term of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "697e3b9f-1195-5622-a1e8-75dfe17b2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529849} {:answer/id #uuid "71a57bcd-4573-5f30-8557-73599f31d7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529869} {:answer/id #uuid "723a838b-c426-5d61-b2ee-cb28f7c35b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529870} {:answer/id #uuid "96c4ae78-46c0-5a05-bf84-a0be16125674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529948} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8024b9-17b3-5634-8f74-ff4ad4a13625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723a838b-c426-5d61-b2ee-cb28f7c35b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529870} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b13fc2-dc9e-51e6-bbb3-0d10c6e2f029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "767866a3-d1d1-5427-8432-b890de342dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101303" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4$ with ${a}_{1}=2,$ then ${a}_{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}=2.$\n\nWe use this formula to compute the terms ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ as follows:\n\n- We first compute ${a}_{2}$ by substituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & ={a}_{n}+4 \\\\ {a}_{1+1} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =(2)+4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${a}_{3}$ by substituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & ={a}_{n}+4 \\\\ {a}_{2+1} & ={a}_{2}+4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =(6)+4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{3}=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbdcd5f-41de-52fa-9652-3084a5edc01f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Second Term of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b30ed1a-6a44-58ff-9882-ad6f74203f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529717} {:answer/id #uuid "6d35d0b7-1f66-5e58-93ff-68c00fb1d98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529856} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd46122-b5fa-5d01-b0e2-04f00999ed7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530131} {:answer/id #uuid "e531a92e-b2e2-511d-a5b3-266cab609785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530153} {:answer/id #u